[[eco-environments]]
=== Environments

[abstract]
An *environment* is one complete Morloc installation managed by `mim`: a
compiler, its runtime, a solved set of language toolchains and packages,
and the programs installed into it. Every other `mim` operation acts on one
environment. This section specifies an environment's identity, where its
files live, how commands run inside it, and what holds when it is changed
or removed.

**Identity.** An environment is a name within a *scope*. The *local* scope
belongs to one user and needs no privileges; the *system* scope is shared by
every user on the machine, and only root may create, change, serve or remove
its environments (any user may run commands in one). A name is 1 to 64
characters of ASCII letters, digits, `-`, `_` and `.`, beginning with a
letter or digit, unique within its scope. A lookup by name tries the local
scope first, so a local environment may shadow a system one of the same
name.

**Backend.** Each environment has one backend, fixed at creation:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Backend | How commands run
| Native | On the host, against a toolchain installed in the environment's own directory.
| Container | In a container started from the environment's own image, by Docker, Podman or Apptainer.
|===

When no backend is named, `mim` chooses native where the host can run it
(glibc Linux, Apple Silicon macOS, or NixOS with `nix` and unprivileged
user namespaces), and otherwise a configured container engine or the only
one installed. With several engines and no configured choice it asks rather
than guessing.

**Files.** Each scope has a configuration root and a data root:

[cols="1,2,2", options="header"]
|===
| Scope | Configuration | Data
| local | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/morloc` (default `~/.config/morloc`) | `$XDG_DATA_HOME/morloc` (default `~/.local/share/morloc`)
| system | `/etc/morloc` | `/usr/local/share/morloc`
|===

An environment's configuration directory holds its settings, its views
(<<eco-serving>>), its extra engine flags and the record of its running
server. It is small, edited by `mim` only, and never visible inside the
environment. The environment exists exactly when its settings record does.

Its data directory holds everything the environment's programs see: the
Morloc home and state (<<eco-overview>>), the solved toolchain and its lock,
the recorded requirements of every program (<<eco-solving>>), the home
directory of its shell, and its server logs. Downloads that environments can
share -- compiler releases, the solver binary, `mim` binaries for other
platforms -- are stored once per scope in the data root and never modified
after they are complete.

**The default environment.** A command that names no environment acts on
the default. Each scope records at most one default, by name. The local
default wins if it names an existing environment; otherwise the system
default applies. There is no activation step and no variable that selects
an environment, so a command means the same thing in every shell.

**Running a command.** `mim run` and `mim shell` execute a command with the
environment's compiler, runtime, toolchain and installed programs first on
the `PATH` and its home as `MORLOC_HOME`. The working directory is the
user's current directory, mounted at `/work` in a container, and files
written there belong to the invoking user. A container sees only the host
variables passed to it explicitly. The command's exit status becomes
`mim`'s.

Three variables mark a command as running in a managed environment, and are
set together:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Variable | Meaning
| `MORLOC_ENV` | Non-empty inside a managed environment. `morloc make` solves packages only when it is set.
| `MORLOC_BUILD_HOOK` | The program `morloc make` calls to solve packages: the `mim` that manages this environment.
| `MORLOC_BIN` | The compiler, so the hook asks the exact compiler that is building for its language requirements.
|===

The hook is `mim` itself, in the same binary as the manager. Staging the
manager's own binary into the environment keeps the two at one version.

Before a solve, a build or a native command, `mim` removes the variables of
any other conda, mamba or pixi environment active in the user's shell.
Their activation scripts add compiler flags and library paths, and mixing
two package worlds produces binaries that link against both.

**Changing an environment.** `mim update` rebuilds an environment and
`mim modify` changes its settings, rebuilding when the toolchain changes.
The Morloc version an environment was provisioned with moves only when an
update asks for another version explicitly; no other command changes it. A
move is refused if an installed module declares a range of Morloc versions
that excludes the target. A change either completes or leaves the
environment's settings, requirements and solved toolchain as they were.

**Removal.** `mim rm` stops the environment's server, then removes its
configuration, data, image and volumes. It leaves alone directories the user
supplied (a host directory mounted as its home) and the shared downloads, so
a later `mim new` does not start from nothing.

